{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments ["<b>He shall see it, and to him I will give it, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say ״יִרְאֶנָּה״ (he shall see it), since He is promising to give it to him — that is included in the general promise. And it appears that HaShem intended to say that two merits were in Caleb's hand regarding this matter, and for their sake he merited two things: first, that he turned himself away from the counsel of the spies and did not speak evil; second, that on the contrary, he refuted the words of the spies in order to follow after HaShem, as it is written (Numbers 13:30): ״וַיַּהַס כָּלֵב״ etc. And HaShem designated for him two good things: corresponding to the fact that he turned himself away from the counsel of the spies, a merit accrued to him that he would not die in their decree — and that is what He prefaced by saying <b>ben Yefunneh, he shall see it</b>, meaning: because he turned (פִּנָּה) himself away from the counsel of the spies, he departed from the category of the decree of ״if a man sees, etc., and all who provoke Me shall not see it״ (Numbers 14:23). And corresponding to the fact that he followed after HaShem, He gave him the land — and that is His saying ״and to him I will give the land, etc., because he followed after HaShem.״"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:14:24.373027808Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}